+Owner skill: `skill://rust-conventions`. Starts one program and pipes its messages both ways in one of two framings (plan §2 #70, #109); never parses a message. `lib.rs` (the public surface; `#![warn(missing_docs)]`), `run.rs` (`Running`: start(program, args, folder, framing, events), send, stop, is_finished, wait; per run a supervisor thread owning the `Child` — stop asked, stdout closed or broken, events dropped, or the program gone, then `STOP_GRACE` 2 s, kill, reap, up to `OUTPUT_DRAIN` 1 s for the readers, one `Exited` last; a writer thread owning stdin; a stdout reader sending `Message`s; a stderr reader sending `Log` lines, whose end stops nothing; `HostEvent`, whose serde/specta shape is the generated `HostEvent` in `bindings.ts`; `CREATE_NO_WINDOW` on Windows), `frame.rs` (`Framing`: `ContentLength` — headers case-insensitive, header lines up to 8 KiB, a bad or too large frame stops the program — or `Lines` — `message + "\n"` out, one line per message in, a trailing `\r` dropped, empty lines skipped, a line over the cap or not UTF-8 dropped with a `Log` line while reading goes on; `MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES` 64 MiB for both; stderr lines cut at 8 KiB), `program.rs` (`find`: a name with a separator relative to the project, else `ProgramFolders` (plan §2 #72): each `project` entry in list order in the project folder and each parent up to the root, then `PATH`, then each `home` entry in the user's home folder (`ProgramPlace::Home`; none without a home folder); an entry that is not plain `/`-separated names (`plain_name`) is skipped; Windows `PATHEXT`; `ProgramFound` is the window's answer shape). std threads and channels only: no `Arc`, no async, no `yamt-*` crate, no tauri, no `serde_json` (arch-check). `src/bin/line-echo.rs` is the line-framed test program `tests/lines.rs` starts as a real child (its commands, incl. `huge`, `bytes`, `burst`, `orphan`, unix `close-stderr`/`close-stdout`, are listed in its header); framing and lookup have unit tests in their files. Tests start the built binaries in place; never exec a file a test just wrote (ETXTBSY, see `lsp-host`'s `place_echo`).
